WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.040
Some anomalies kill for fun, others for food,&nbsp;
others because they simply have no choice. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.320
the other side of the coin, some want nothing&nbsp;
more than to spread love, joy, and happiness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.160
But it’s rare that you see an anomaly&nbsp;
that has genuinely pure intentions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:22.400
but whose attempts to do good often turn deadly.&nbsp;
One such figure, and the subject of today’s tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:29.040
is SCP-049 - The Plague Doctor.
If you’re at all familiar with SCP Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.760
then you will have likely heard of the infamous&nbsp;
Plague Doctor. For those of you who haven’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.840
here’s a bit of context for SCP-049.
He appears exactly as you’d expect a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.640
plague doctor from the late medieval period&nbsp;
or renaissance periods. A tall figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:51.200
shrouded in long black robes, a hood over his head&nbsp;
with only a venetian beaked mask protruding from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.480
underneath. These aren’t just a fashion statement&nbsp;
either, the SCP Foundation discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:03.440
these are all part of SCP-049’s physical body,&nbsp;
sharing a similar structure to muscle and bone.&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.240
The Plague Doctor’s primary goal is to find those&nbsp;
he considers to be infected with something he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.760
refers to as The Pestilence. If he ever senses&nbsp;
this affliction, he will attempt to make direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:20.320
skin contact with the person carrying it.&nbsp;
Why? Because SCP-049’s touch is lethal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.520
killing anyone he comes into contact with.
You might think that’s all the Plague Doctor does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.280
goes from person to person, killing them with his&nbsp;
deadly touch, but you would be wrong. Once he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.160
killed a victim, SCP-049 then begins performing a&nbsp;
crude, outdated form of surgery on their corpse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.680
Using equipment he produces from his mysterious&nbsp;
and apparently bottomless magical medical satchel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.200
he injects a number of unknown chemicals into&nbsp;
the corpse, which brings them back to life and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:57.920
turns them into an instance of SCP-049-2; or&nbsp;
in other words, a vicious, mindless zombie.&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.960
At present, SCP-049 is kept secure in a&nbsp;
holding cell by the Foundation, under watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:09.760
by surveillance cameras for twenty-four hours a&nbsp;
day. And that is where our story begins. The tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:16.640
of a deadly, dangerous doctor, and his days spent&nbsp;
in captivity. Locked away in a cage, contained by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:24.880
the SCP Foundation, there he sits, alone. It is a&nbsp;
tale known only by the simple title: “Going Home.”&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.240
It begins with the Plague Doctor, left alone in&nbsp;
his cell. He had been there for such a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.800
that he’d forgotten exactly how long. But there he&nbsp;
sat, taunted by the sensation of pestilence around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:43.440
him. It was a small, almost undetectable feeling,&nbsp;
but it reeked of decay and disease, hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.920
in the very air particles of SCP-049’s cell.
Although they had long since stopped the practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.640
the SCP Foundation used to send its doctors and&nbsp;
researchers into 049’s cell, bringing him cadavers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.920
as test subjects. The Plague Doctor would use&nbsp;
these specimens to hone his craft, perfecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:09.040
his method of transforming the dead into instances&nbsp;
of SCP-049-2. But, of course, the doctors working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.480
for the Foundation weren’t exactly free of disease&nbsp;
themselves and they too often needed to be cured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:21.840
by his touch, and soon they stopped visiting&nbsp;
SCP-049 in person, leaving him alone in his cell.&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.480
Even these so-called people of science&nbsp;
feared the true cure, it seemed.&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.880
They would talk to the Plague Doctor via&nbsp;
an intercom system, interviewing him and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.960
asking questions about what he did or how&nbsp;
he felt. One question stood out to SCP-049:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.240
the Foundation researchers had asked him if he&nbsp;
had any regrets for what he had done to people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.280
The Plague Doctor thought it foolish to even ask.
And then, even the questions coming through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.800
intercom stopped after a while. For a&nbsp;
time, SCP-049 was happy in his cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.320
left alone with his thoughts, safe from the&nbsp;
diseases of the outside world. The Plague Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:05.360
was at peace for a change, resting in comfort.&nbsp;
Until the familiar smell of pestilence returned.&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.920
The presence of disease drove him to attempt&nbsp;
an escape. Producing his medical implements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:16.160
from the bag he kept concealed in his robes,&nbsp;
SCP-049 tried to pry open the door to his cell.&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.520
He wasn’t sure how long it took, the passage of&nbsp;
time had long since stopped registering to him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.760
But eventually, he had it open, just&nbsp;
enough so that he could pass through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.960
out into the corridor beyond the chamber he&nbsp;
had been held in. There was no one else around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:37.200
no doctors or researchers or Foundation security&nbsp;
personnel. No one to stop the Plague Doctor from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.160
following the stench of the disease that had&nbsp;
driven him to find a way out of containment.&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.600
Navigating through the hallways of the Foundation&nbsp;
facility, SCP-049 tracked the source of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.520
disease to a locker. With little trouble,&nbsp;
he opened it, finding a metallic box within.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.200
As he studied the container, his fingers found the&nbsp;
button that opened it. It hissed as the gases kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.320
the inside of the box mixed with the air. The cold&nbsp;
vapor hit the Plague Doctor in the face, catching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.600
him by surprise and making him drop the box.
Reasserting himself, the Plague Doctor bent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.720
over and picked up the contents of the&nbsp;
refrigerated container: a single mosquito.&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.800
It wasn’t the insect itself that had drawn&nbsp;
SCP-049 out of his cell. True, mosquitos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.080
are known to spread diseases like malaria, but it&nbsp;
was what was inside this mosquito that the Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:33.360
wanted to eradicate. Its blood sac was full,&nbsp;
swollen with dark fluid that SCP-049 knew to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.120
infected. Using the metal box as an improvised&nbsp;
operating table, he produced his bag of medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.640
implements, and the doctor began to operate.
He retrieved a syringe from his bag, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:50.320
vial filled with thick, black liquid. Half filling&nbsp;
the syringe with the liquid, he plunged the needle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.760
into the mosquito, and extracted the blood that&nbsp;
the insect was carrying. The infected bodily fluid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:01.440
mixed with the medicine in the doctor’s syringe,&nbsp;
leaving the bug as little more than a dry husk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.400
The Plague Doctor watched as the two substances&nbsp;
mixed together, before stabbing it back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.840
the mosquito, filling the creature with the&nbsp;
combination of both liquids. He refused to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:17.280
any trace of pestilence in the insect’s body.
The mosquito twitched itself back to life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:23.520
looking around until it saw SCP-049 observing it.
“Who’s there?” it asked. “Where is Merle?”&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.760
“There is no one here but me, and you,” the&nbsp;
doctor explained. “You are in a prison. Though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.720
the wardens, it seems, have left.”
“A prison?” the insect responded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.760
“Oh god! I’m at Site 19!”
Naturally, it seemed rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:44.080
strange for a mosquito to be able to talk, let&nbsp;
alone know about the highly classified Site 19.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.640
But, as is always the case in the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;
the strange always has an anomalous explanation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:57.520
You see, this mosquito was not like any other.&nbsp;
For one, she even had a name; it was Leslie.&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:05.680
As it turned out, Leslie was a part of an SCP,&nbsp;
one of the Foundation’s own creation: SCP-3774.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.400
These were a genetically and cybernetically&nbsp;
enhanced species of mosquito. Why would the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.200
Foundation spend time and resources creating&nbsp;
something like this? Originally, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:21.360
designed as an undetectable, covert way of keeping&nbsp;
tabs on Persons of Interest to the SCP Foundation.&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.000
The Plague Doctor thought it odd that, after&nbsp;
having spent so much time imprisoned there, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.680
he should finally learn the name of Site 19 from&nbsp;
a mosquito, of all things. Leslie seemed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:37.120
in a state of panic, however, urgently insisting&nbsp;
that she had to get back to someone named Merle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:43.200
SCP-049 had no clue what she meant, he’d never&nbsp;
encountered anyone that went by that name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.840
and had no idea where to even begin looking.
“I know where he lives, I can find it,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:51.680
the mosquito insisted. “I just… I don’t&nbsp;
know how I could ever get out of here.”&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:57.040
She was wary of the creature she found herself&nbsp;
talking to. Despite being a genetically engineered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:03.760
insect, Leslie was, for all intents and purposes,&nbsp;
an agent of the SCP Foundation. And now, she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.200
currently conversing with an anomaly that she knew&nbsp;
nothing about. But he wasn’t trying to harm her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.200
in fact, whatever the Plague Doctor&nbsp;
had done seemed to have revived her.&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:19.040
“I have gotten myself this far, and now that I&nbsp;
have, I have no further reason to stay here.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.640
The doctor announced, holding a&nbsp;
hand out for Leslie to fly up to.&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.840
“Then… then you’ll help me get out of here?” she&nbsp;
asked, readying her wings and landing in his palm.&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:33.280
“I will,” SCP-049 agreed.
The pair introduced themselves properly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:39.200
Leslie giving SCP-049 her name while he just&nbsp;
instructed her to call him “doctor”, before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:44.800
set out together. After almost a week of traveling&nbsp;
on foot through rugged terrain and harsh winds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:49.680
the Plague Doctor and his insect companion&nbsp;
had yet to encounter another living soul.&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.960
Thick, dark clouds hung in the sky above them,&nbsp;
and Leslie soon found it hard to remember what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.360
the sun looked like. She feared that&nbsp;
she might even forget Merle’s face.&nbsp;

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.480
“We’re close.” she said. “Just&nbsp;
a few more miles, and then…”&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:07.120
“And then we will arrive,”&nbsp;
SCP-049 finished her sentence.&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.920
“Tell me, doctor, why did you come all this way&nbsp;
with me?” Leslie the mosquito asked, “You could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.800
have left when we got out, gone your own way.”
In response he gave one simple sentence:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.280
“Merely because I wanted to.”
Finally, the intrepid pair arrived at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:28.320
a house once belonging to Merle. It was abandoned,&nbsp;
ramshackle, in a total state of disrepair. Decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:33.440
had passed since anyone had set foot in the place.
The living room that had once been so familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.320
to Leslie had now been transformed into a&nbsp;
barren, empty space. The sight of it made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.680
her so distraught with sadness that she would&nbsp;
have been sick, if mosquitoes could be sick.&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.800
Merle, whoever he was or had&nbsp;
been, was now nowhere to be found.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:53.760
One of Leslie’s cybernetic enhancements was&nbsp;
an internal positioning system, giving her all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:59.680
sorts of data and readouts, including a clock.&nbsp;
It told her just how long she’d been away for.&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:04.080
“I’ve been dead for two hundred years?”&nbsp;
the mosquito cried in the palm of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.840
doctor’s hand. “Then… then Merle is…&nbsp;
is dead. Everyone… everyone is dead.”&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:15.120
She kept repeating it to herself, over and over as&nbsp;
if she was trying to convince herself of what had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.800
happened. Eventually, the words lost all meaning.&nbsp;
As he held Leslie in his hands, perhaps the Plague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:26.480
Doctor felt sorry for the mosquito. After all,&nbsp;
the Plague Doctor had always had a respectful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:31.760
polite personality despite his frightening&nbsp;
exterior. He had always believed that he’d acted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:38.160
in service of the greater good, trying to cure the&nbsp;
world of pestilence, to bring the end of disease.&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.760
He only ever killed because, to him, it was in&nbsp;
his victim’s best interest for him to perform his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:49.520
procedures. To his captors at the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;
the doctor had been courteous and co-operative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:54.880
and he acted the same towards his fellow&nbsp;
SCPs. Not just Leslie, but even the sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.520
of anomalies with nothing but hatred in their&nbsp;
hearts. The doctor saw them as just further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:05.920
victims of the pestilence, in need of curing.
SCP-049 sat in silence for a while with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:11.280
mosquito friend, letting her mourn the loss&nbsp;
of Merle and everything she had ever known.&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:15.840
“Where will you go now?” he asked.
Still in tears, barely able to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:21.440
through her sobs, Leslie decided that she wanted&nbsp;
to stay where she was. The insect wanted to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:27.120
where Merle had lived, in a desperate attempt to&nbsp;
recapture the world that she once knew. Sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:32.800
in the Plague Doctor’s gloved hand, still sobbing&nbsp;
even though she had run out of tears, Leslie said:&nbsp;

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.320
“Any minute… any minute Merle&nbsp;
will come through the door.”&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:41.040
The Plague Doctor held his tiny companion&nbsp;
where she was, while she grieved.&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.200
“Then I will wait for him,”&nbsp;
he told her, “here with you.”&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:51.840
Now go check out “SCP-049 - the Plague Doctor&nbsp;
Captured” and “SCP-049 - What Actually is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:57.840
Pestilence? The Plague Doctor Questions and&nbsp;
Theories” for more on this enigmatic medicine man!

